# ZONE C. Sourced from ~/.bash_profile, behind an interactive guard.
#
# Runs ONLY for interactive LOGIN shells — the shell handed to you at the top
# of a session:
#   Ghostty window or tab          macOS runs it via login(1), execs `bash -l`
#   new tmux pane/window/session   default-command empty => tmux runs login shell
#   `ssh longreads`
#
# Not run for shells spawned from inside one of those (`bash` at a prompt,
# `sudo bash`, a subshell), nor for non-interactive-ssh+ext.
#
# KEEP THE GUARD in bash_profile: `[[ $- == *i* ]]`. ~/.bash_profile is read by
# ALL login shells, interactive or not, so without it `bash -lc 'cmd'` runs this
# file and anything that prints lands in cmd's stdout. That was a real bug on
# longreads (the df greeting); see the decision log, 260822.
#
# Nearly everything you sit in front of IS a login shell, so this zone is thin
# on purpose. If you also want it in `sudo bash` and subshells, it belongs one
# zone up in all/main-interactive.
#
#   -> "Is it for a human typing, but nested shells should skip it?"
#
# MORE INFO: bash/README.Interactive-Soup.md

# ------

# Good place for things that would break scp, or that should not repeat in a
# nested shell. For example, on longreads a df runs at login:
#   df -h /JBOD /storage
# That is NOT run by `ssh longreads whoami`, nor by `sudo bash` inside a
# session — only when a login shell is handed to you.

shopt -s nullglob
# Load files specific to "interactive login" shells.
[ -n "${DOTFILES:-}" ] && for f in $DOTFILES/*/interactive-login $DOTFILES/*/*/interactive-login ; do source "$f" ; done
shopt -u nullglob
